"The algebra of the monoid of order-preserving functions on an n-set and other reduced E-Fountain semigroups | Semigroup Forum | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-025-10595-2)\nciteturn10170view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view0\",\"lineno\":421}); Total lines: 800\nL416: As a conclusion, we observe that there is a one-to-one correspondence between functions \\(f\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and pairs of sets \\(X,Y\\subseteq [n]\\) such that \\(|X|=|Y|\\) and \\(n\\in X\\). From now on we denote by \\(f_{Y,X}\\) the function associated with \\(X,Y\\subseteq [n]\\) (in contrary to [cite42†27 , Section 4.2] where this function was denoted \\(f_{X,Y})\\).\nL417: Note that \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\textsf{im}}\\,}}(f_{Y,X})=Y\\) (where \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\textsf{im}}\\,}}(f)\\) is the image of f) and X contains the maximal elements of the kernel classes of \\(f_{Y,X}\\). Another easy observation is that \\(f_{Z,Y}f_{Y,X}=f_{Z,X}\\).\nL418: The monoid \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) is a regular submonoid of \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {T}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and its Green’s equivalence relations are well-known (see [cite65†6 , Section 2]). In particular:\nL419: $$\\begin{aligned} f_{Y_{1},X_{1}}\\,{{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {L}}\\,}}\\,f_{Y_{2},X_{2}}&\\iff \\ker (f_{Y_{1},X_{1}})=\\ker (f_{Y_{2},X_{2}})\\iff X_{1}=X_{2}\\\\ f_{Y_{1},X_{1}}\\,{{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {R}}\\,}}\\,f_{Y_{2},X_{2}}&\\iff {{\\,\\mathrm{\\textsf{im}}\\,}}(f_{Y_{1},X_{1}})={{\\,\\mathrm{\\textsf{im}}\\,}}(f_{Y_{2},X_{2}})\\iff Y_{1}=Y_{2} \\end{aligned}$$\nL420: We want to define our distinguished set of idempotents E. Note that for every \\(X\\subseteq [n]\\) with \\(n\\in X\\) the function \\(f_{X,X}\\) is an idempotent that can be described by \\(f_{X,X}(i)=\\min \\{x\\in X\\mid i\\le x\\}\\). It will be convenient to define \\(e_{X}=f_{X,X}\\) where \\(X\\subseteq [n]\\) with \\(n\\in X\\) and we set \\(E=\\{e_{X}\\mid n\\in X\\}\\) (this is precisely the set of idempotents of the Catalan monoid — see [cite26†29 , Section 17.5.2]).\nL421: We have just seen that \\(f_{Y,X}{{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {L}}\\,}}e_{X}\\) and since \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {L}}\\,}}\\subseteq \\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\) we have \\(f_{Y,X}\\,\\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\,e_{X}\\). On the other hand, it is not always true that \\(f_{Y,X}\\,{{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {R}}\\,}}\\,e_{Y}\\) because \\(e_{Y}\\) is defined only if \\(n\\in Y\\).\nL422: Denote by \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) the submonoid of \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) of all order-preserving functions such that \\(f(n)=n\\) and note that \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}=\\{f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\mid n\\in Y\\}\\). This monoid was discussed in detail in [cite42†27 , Section 4.2]. We have \\(f_{Y,X}\\,{{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {R}}\\,}}\\,e_{Y}\\) if only if \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\).\nL423: However, it is easy to see that\nL424: $$ e_{Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{Y,X} $$\nL425: if and only if \\(Y\\subseteq Z\\). Therefore, the minimal left identity of \\(f_{Y,X}\\) from E is \\(e_{Y\\cup \\{n\\}}\\) hence \\(f_{Y,X}\\,\\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E\\,e_{Y\\cup \\{n\\}}\\). It will be convenient to set \\(\\widetilde{Y}=Y\\cup \\{n\\}\\) for every \\(Y\\subseteq [n]\\) so \\(f_{Y,X}\\,\\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E\\,e_{\\widetilde{Y}}\\) for every \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\).\nL426: The first conclusion is that there exists an idempotent of E in every \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\)-class and \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E\\)-class of \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\). It is also easy to check that \\(e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{X}\\iff e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}\\iff X\\subseteq Y\\) (see also [cite32†26 , Section 6] where we derived this fact from [cite66†2 , Lemma 3.6]) so \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) is a reduced E-Fountain semigroup.\nL427: Since \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {L}}\\,}}\\subseteq \\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\) and there is a unique element of E in every \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\)-class we deduce that \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {L}}\\,}}=\\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\) and therefore \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal {L}}_E\\) is a right congruence so \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) satisfies the right congruence condition.\nL428: We will see later that \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\) does not always imply that \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=(f_{W,Z})^{+}\\) so in view of Corollary cite57†3.6 we deduce that \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) does not satisfy the generalized right ample condition. However, it turns out that it satisfies the generalized left ample condition.\nL429: Generalized left ample condition and isomorphism of algebras\nL430: \nL431: First, we state the dual of the relevant results from Sect. cite41†3 . Every \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E\\)-class of a reduced E-Fountain semigroup S is an incomplete right S-action and a partial function\nL432: \nL433: $$ l_{a}:\\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E(a^{*})\\rightarrow \\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E(a^{+}) $$\nL434: \nL435: is defined by\nL436: \nL437: $$ l_{a}(b)={\\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} ab & (ab)^{+}=a^{+}\\\\ \\text {undefined} & \\text {otherwise}. \\end{array}\\right. } $$\nL438: The dual of Proposition cite67†3.3 is:\nL439: ### Proposition 5.1\nL440: \nL441: Let S be a reduced E-Fountain semigroup. The following conditions are equivalent.\nL442: \nL443:   1. 1.\nL444: \nL445: The semigroup S satisfies \\((((ab)^{+}a)^{*}b)^{+}=((ab)^{+}a)^{*}\\) for every \\(a,b\\in S\\).\nL446: \nL447:   2. 2.\nL448: \nL449: The semigroup S satisfies \\((ab)^{+}=a^{+}\\implies (a^{*}b)^{+}=a^{*}\\) for every \\(a,b\\in S\\).\nL450: \nL451:   3. 3.\nL452: \nL453: The partial function \\(l_{a}:\\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E(a^{*})\\rightarrow \\widetilde{\\mathcal {R}}_E(a^{+})\\) is a partial homomorphism of incomplete right S-actions for every \\(a\\in S\\).\nL454: Define a relation \\(\\trianglelefteq _{r}\\) on S by the rule that \\(a\\trianglelefteq _{r}b\\) if and only if \\(a=eb\\) for some \\(e\\in E\\). If we assume that \\(\\mathcal {C}(S)\\) is a category and the relation \\(\\trianglelefteq _{r}\\) is principally finite we can define a function \\(\\varphi :\\Bbbk S\\rightarrow \\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)\\) on basis elements by\nL455: \nL456: $$ \\varphi (a)=\\sum _{c\\trianglelefteq _{r}a}C(c). $$\nL457: \nL458: The dual of Theorem cite60†3.12 is:\nL459: ### Theorem 5.2\nL460: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe algebra of the monoid of order-preserving functions on an n-set and other reduced E-Fountain semigroups | Semigroup Forum | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-025-10595-2)\nciteturn10170view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view0\",\"lineno\":556}); Total lines: 800\nL551: ### Proof\nL552: If \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then Lemma cite80†5.9 implies \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W,X}\\) so \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=e_{\\widetilde{W}}=(f_{W,Z})^{+}\\). If \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W^{\\prime },X}\\) by Lemma cite79†5.10 so \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=e_{\\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}}\\) where \\((f_{W,Z})^{+}=e_{\\widetilde{W}}\\).\nL553: If \\(f_{W,Z}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(\\max (W)=n\\) and \\(W^{\\prime }=W\\backslash \\{n\\}\\) so \\(\\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}=W=\\widetilde{W}\\) and we obtain \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=(f_{W,Z})^{+}\\). If \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(\\max (W)\\ne n\\) so \\(\\max (W)\\notin \\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}\\) hence \\(\\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}\\ne \\widetilde{W}\\) and \\({(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}\\ne (f_{W,Z})^{+}}\\). \\(\\square \\)\nL554: The graph \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) associated with \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) can be described as follows. The objects are in one-to-one correspondence with the set of idempotents \\(E=\\{e_{X}\\}\\) (where \\(X\\subseteq [n]\\) such that \\(n\\in X\\)). For every \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) we associate a non-zero morphism \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) from \\(e_{X}\\) to \\(e_{\\widetilde{Y}}\\).\nL555: Before we turn to describe the composition we emphasize an important observation. Let \\(X,Y\\subseteq [n]\\) such that \\(n\\in X,Y\\). If \\(|Y|=|X|\\) then \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) is the unique non-zero morphism from \\(e_{X}\\) to \\(e_{Y}\\) (note that \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) in this case).\nL556: If \\(|Y|=|X|+1\\) then \\(C(f_{Y^{\\prime },X})\\) is the unique non-zero morphism from \\(e_{X}\\) to \\(e_{Y}\\) (note that \\(Y^{\\prime }=Y\\backslash \\{n\\}\\) and \\(f_{Y^{\\prime },X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) in this case). If \\(|Y|\\notin \\{|X|,|X|+1\\}\\) then there are no non-zero morphisms from \\(e_{X}\\) to \\(e_{Y}\\).\nL557: ### Example 5.13\nL558: \nL559: The graph \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{3})\\) has the four objects \\(e_{\\{3\\}},e_{\\{1,3\\}},e_{\\{2,3\\}},e_{\\{1,2,3\\}}\\) and 10 non-zero morphisms. If we naturally draw \\(e_{Y}\\) above \\(e_{X}\\) if \\(|Y|>|X|\\) and ignore the identity and zero morphisms then the graph \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{3})\\) is the following graph:\nL560: \nL561: cite81†Image: figure a†media.springernature.com L562: Recall that composition in \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) of two non-zero morphisms C(g), C(f) with \\(g^{*}=f^{+}\\) is defined by:\nL563: \nL564: $$ C(g)\\cdot C(f)={\\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} C(gf) & (gf)^{*}=f^{*},\\quad (gf)^{+}=g^{+}\\\\ 0 & \\text {otherwise}. \\end{array}\\right. } $$\nL565: We can simplify this description. Since \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) satisfies the right congruence condition, \\(g^{*}=f^{+}\\implies (gf)^{*}=f^{*}\\) so we can drop the requirement for \\((gf)^{*}=f^{*}\\). From Lemma cite82†5.12 we deduce that \\(g^{*}=f^{+}\\) implies \\((gf)^{+}=g^{+}\\) if at least one of g, f is in \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and \\((gf)^{+}\\ne g^{+}\\) otherwise. Therefore, we obtain:\nL566: ### Lemma 5.14\nL567: \nL568: Composition in \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) of two non-zero morphisms C(g), C(f) with \\(g^{*}=f^{+}\\) can be described by\nL569: \nL570: $$ C(g)\\cdot C(f)={\\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} C(gf) & f\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\text { or }g\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\\\ 0 & \\text {otherwise}. \\end{array}\\right. } $$\nL571: \nL572: Maximal semisimple image\nL573: We finish this subsection with an observation on the maximal semisimple image of the algebra \\(\\Bbbk {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) where \\(\\Bbbk \\) is a field. Let \\(D_{n}\\) be the subcategory of \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) with the same set of objects but the morphisms are only morphisms of the form \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) where \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\).\nL574: Note that if \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(\\widetilde{Y}=Y\\) (since \\(n\\in Y\\)) and if \\(Z=Y\\) then \\(C(f_{W,Z})\\cdot C(f_{Y,X})=C(f_{W,X})\\) where \\(f_{W,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\). Therefore, \\(D_{n}\\) is indeed a subcategory (without zero morphisms) of \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\). Moreover, every morphism \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) in \\(D_{n}\\) has an inverse \\(C(f_{X,Y})\\) so \\(D_{n}\\) is a groupoid.\nL575: Note also that the identity morphisms \\(C(e_{X})\\) (for \\(X\\subseteq [n]\\) with \\(n\\in X\\)) are the only endomorphisms in \\(D_{n}\\).\nL576: ### Lemma 5.15\nL577: \nL578: Let \\(\\Bbbk \\) be a field. The Jacobson radical of \\(\\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) is the subvector space spanned by all the morphisms of the form \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) where \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) (or equivalently, \\(n\\notin Y)\\) and\nL579: \nL580: $$ \\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})/{{\\,\\textrm{Rad}\\,}}(\\Bbbk \\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}))\\simeq \\Bbbk D_{n}. $$\nL581: ### Proof\nL582: \nL583: Denote by R the subvector space of \\(\\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) spanned by all the morphisms of the form \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) where \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\). First note that according to Lemma cite83†5.14 if \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then\nL584: \nL585: $$ C(f_{W,Z})\\cdot C(f_{Y,X})=0. $$\nL586: Moreover, if \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and \\(f_{W,Z}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) are such that \\(C(f_{W,Z})\\cdot C(f_{Y,X})\\ne 0\\) then \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) according to Lemma cite84†5.11 (and the dual holds if \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\cdot C(f_{W,Z})\\ne 0\\)). Both facts together imply that R is a nilpotent ideal (of nilpotency degree 2).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHomomorphisms of Hecke–Kiselman Monoids Associated to Simple Oriented Graphs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.15497v3)\nciteturn10170view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view2\",\"lineno\":350}); Total lines: 684\nL335: If $x_{i}\\in R$ and $x_{j}\\in X\\setminus Y$, then by the definition of $R$, there exist $x_{b}\\in Y\\setminus X$, $r\\in\\mathbb{N}_{0}$, and $x_{i_{1}},\\dots,x_{i_{r}}\\in I$ such that\nL336:  | $$x_{b}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i_{1}}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}\\dots\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i_{r}}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{j}.$$  |\nL337: \nL338: Since $x_{i}\\in R\\subseteq I$ and $x_{j}\\in X\\setminus Y$, this contradicts the fact that $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ holds. If $x_{i}\\in R$ and $x_{j}\\in S$, we have some $x_{b}\\in Y\\setminus X$, $r\\in\\mathbb{N}_{0}$, and $x_{i_{1}},\\dots,x_{i_{r}}\\in I$ such that\nL339:  | $$x_{b}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i_{1}}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}\\dots\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i_{r}}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{j}.$$  |\nL340: \nL341: Since $x_{i}\\in R\\subseteq I$, this implies that $x_{j}\\in R$. Thus $x_{j}\\in R\\cap S$, which is again a contradiction. The above cases are exhaustive by (cite46†1 ). Hence, in all cases, we have reached a contradiction, and therefore there are no arrows starting in $M$ and ending in $N$. ∎\nL342: Since $N\\subseteq X\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$ and $M\\subseteq Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, Remark cite47†2.6 implies $N,M\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. Since $N\\cap M=\\emptyset$ and there are no arrows starting in $M$ and ending in $N$, Lemma cite48†2.13 yields\nL343: \nL344:  | $$N\\cup M\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad e_{N}e_{M}=e_{N\\cup M}.$$  |\nL345: \nL346: Since $X\\cup Y=N\\cup M$ by (cite49†2 ), we obtain\nL347: \nL348:  | $$X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad e_{N}e_{M}=e_{X\\cup Y}.$$  |  | (3)\nL349: Since $R,S\\subseteq X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, we also have $R,S\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$ by Remark cite47†2.6 . As $R\\subseteq M$ and there are no arrows starting in $M$ and ending in $N$, there are no arrows starting in $R$ and ending in $N$. Since $N\\cap R=\\emptyset$, Lemma cite48†2.13 implies\nL350: \nL351:  | $$e_{N}e_{R}=e_{N\\cup R}=e_{X}.$$  |  | (4)\nL352: \nL353: Similarly, since $S\\subseteq N$, there are no arrows starting in $M$ and ending in $S$. Since $S\\cap M=\\emptyset$, Lemma cite48†2.13 implies\nL354:  | $$e_{S}e_{M}=e_{S\\cup M}=e_{Y}.$$  |  | (5)\nL355: \nL356: Since $c(e_{S})=S\\subseteq X$ and $c(e_{R})=R\\subseteq M$, Lemma cite39†2.9 implies\nL357: \nL358:  | $$e_{X}e_{S}=e_{X}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad e_{R}e_{M}=e_{M}.$$  |  | (6)\nL359: \nL360: Using (cite50†3 ), (cite51†4 ), (cite52†5 ), and (cite53†6 ), we obtain\nL361: \nL362:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{S}e_{M}=e_{X}e_{M}=e_{N}e_{R}e_{M}=e_{N}e_{M}=e_{X\\cup Y}.$$  |\nL363: \nL364: This concludes the proof. ∎\nL365: \nL366: Propositions cite54†3.4 and cite55†3.5 imply the following theorem.\nL367: ###### Theorem 3.6.\nL368: \nL369: Let $X,Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. Then $e_{X}e_{Y}$ is an idempotent in $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$ if and only if $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ holds.\nL370: \nL371: ###### Lemma 3.7.\nL372: \nL373: Let $X,Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$ and assume that $X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$ as well. Then $e_{X\\cup Y}e_{X}$ and $e_{Y}e_{X\\cup Y}$ are idempotents in $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$.\nL374: ###### Proof.\nL375: \nL376: Since $X\\setminus(X\\cup Y)=\\emptyset$ and $Y\\setminus(X\\cup Y)=\\emptyset$, conditions $p_{\\Theta}(X\\cup Y,X)$ and $p_{\\Theta}(Y,X\\cup Y)$ vacuously hold. The conclusion follows from Theorem cite56†3.6 . ∎\nL377: \nL378: ###### Theorem 3.8.\nL379: \nL380: Let $X,Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. Then $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ holds if and only if\nL381: \nL382:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}.$$  |\nL383: ###### Proof.\nL384: \nL385: Assume\nL386: \nL387:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}$$  |\nL388: \nL389: holds. Then\nL390: \nL391:  | $$(e_{X}e_{Y})^{2}=e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=(e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X})e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y},$$  |\nL392: \nL393: which implies that $e_{X}e_{Y}$ is an idempotent, and hence by Theorem cite56†3.6 , $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ holds.\nL394: \nL395: Conversely, assume $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ holds. By Theorem cite56†3.6 , $e_{X}e_{Y}$ is an idempotent. By Proposition cite44†2.8 , it follows that $X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$ and\nL396:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X\\cup Y}.$$  |  | (7)\nL397: \nL398: Since $X,Y,X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, Lemma cite57†3.7 implies that $e_{X\\cup Y}e_{X}$ and $e_{Y}e_{X\\cup Y}$ are idempotents as well. By Proposition cite44†2.8 , we further have\nL399: \nL400:  | $$e_{X\\cup Y}e_{X}=e_{(X\\cup Y)\\cup X}=e_{X\\cup Y}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad e_{Y}e_{X\\cup Y}=e_{Y\\cup(X\\cup Y)}=e_{X\\cup Y}.$$  |\nL401: \nL402: Using (cite58†7 ), we conclude that\nL403: \nL404:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}.$$  |\nL405: \nL406: ∎\nL407: ###### Corollary 3.9.\nL408: \nL409: Let $X,Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. Then $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ and $p_{\\Theta}(Y,X)$ both hold if and only if $e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{Y}e_{X}$.\nL410: ###### Proof.\nL411: \nL412: If $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ and $p_{\\Theta}(Y,X)$ both hold, then by Theorem cite59†3.8 , we have\nL413: \nL414:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X},$$  |\nL415: \nL416: which implies $e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{Y}e_{X}$.\nL417: \nL418: Conversely, if $e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{Y}e_{X}$, then\nL419: \nL420:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{X}(e_{Y}e_{X})=e_{X}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{Y}e_{X},$$  |\nL421: \nL422: and\nL423: \nL424:  | $$e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=(e_{Y}e_{X})e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{Y}e_{X},$$  |\nL425: \nL426: which implies\nL427:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad e_{Y}e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X}e_{Y}e_{X}=e_{Y}e_{X}.$$  |\nL428: \nL429: Theorem cite59†3.8 implies that $p_{\\Theta}(X,Y)$ and $p_{\\Theta}(Y,X)$ both hold. ∎\nL430: ## 4 Homomorphisms from $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Gamma}$ to $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$\nL431: \nL432: In this section, we study the set $\\mathrm{Hom}(\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Gamma},\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta})$ of all monoid homomorphisms from $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Gamma}$ to $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$.\nL433: ###### Theorem 4.1.\nL434: \nL435: Let $\\varphi:\\{y_{1},y_{2},\\dots,y_{m}\\}\\to\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$ be a map defined on the generators of $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Gamma}$. The map $\\varphi$ extends to a monoid homomorphism from $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Gamma}$ to $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$ if and only if there exist sets $X_{1},X_{2},\\dots,X_{m}$ with $X_{i}\\subseteq V_{\\Theta}$ such that, for all $i,j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,m\\}$, the following conditions hold:\nL436: \nL437:   1. (i)\nL438: \nL439: $X_{i}\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$;\nL440: \nL441:   2. (ii)\nL442: \nL443: $\\varphi(y_{i})=e_{X_{i}}$;\n"
